※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none
※ pl4g-test: args --conditions=ignore

※ `--conditions=ignore` emits no check at all, so the same program that exits 89
※ with the default semantic runs through and answers.
※
※ What a build turns off is the check and not the checking: the clause is still
※ read, still has to be a `bool`, still has to be pure, and its names still have
※ to mean something.  So a build with conditions off accepts exactly the
※ programs a build with them on accepts, which is the one property that makes
※ the switch safe to have -- nothing compiles only because the checks were off.

let zero: u8 = 0u8

@[inline(never)]
fn twice(a: u8) → u8 pre(a > 0u8) post(⎕answer > a):
    a ⊞ a

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(twice(zero), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
